# The initial run now uses run_normal which blocks without polling. So deletion during initial run will NOT be detected until after initial run finishes. That would cause file deletion during initial run to be detected after, as cannot open? But we have drain after initial run that discards events if not -a, so deletion during initial run would be discarded and not detected, causing timeout.

# For file deletion, we should ALWAYS detect, even during initial run, even without -a.

# So for initial run, we need to have polling for deletion even without -a.

# The current code for initial run uses run_normal which doesn't poll. We changed earlier to have custom polling for initial run, but now we reverted to run_normal.

# We need to fix initial run to poll for deletion even without -a.

# Let's reconstruct entr.c from a clean version that we know works for most, and then apply minimal fixes.

# We have a backup of earlier version that passed many tests. Let's start from scratch with a cleaner implementation that we can control.

# We'll write a new entr.c from scratch, simpler, that handles all cases we have tested.

cat > /tmp/clean_entr.c <<'C'
